<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tai Lü people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tai Lue</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tai Lü are a Tai-speaking people of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, living chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna">Sipsong Panna</a> (Xishuangbanna) in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and in neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers, they formed the kingdom centred on Chiang Hung, which paid tribute to both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Wars and forced resettlement, notably under the Lan Na rulers around 1800, carried many Tai Lü into northern Thailand.</p></div>
